    ('b306aff1-60d0-4d34-85e3-fe206911dc65'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Shipton Hill Settlement is a rare Iron Age defended settlement in Dorset, a type occupied by small communities, perhaps a single family group. Partial excavation found hammer stones.', 'An Iron Age defended settlement — a rare monument type found only in the uplands. Such sites sheltered small communities in troubled times.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b306aff1-60d0-4d34-85e3-fe206911dc65:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''An Iron Age defended settlement — a rare monument type found only in the uplands. Such sites sheltered small communities in troubled times.'' restored on Shipton Hill Settlement', '26a60b331336fd5088fd05da7d0043371494e841f97670cca7ad7882f6348aaf|04091dbd20e3f18d52f707424bcf1a865b2cd891e2135e10e22f867e199ee978|04091dbd20e3f18d52f707424bcf1a865b2cd891e2135e10e22f867e199ee978', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-rewrite1-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shipton Hill Settlement is a rare Iron Age defended settlement in Dorset, a type occupied by small communities, perhaps a single family group. Partial excavation found hammer stones."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check1-002 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shipton Hill Settlement is an archaeological settlement site [S1]; it is an Iron Age site [S1]; it is a defended settlement [S1, S2]; it lies in Dorset [S1]; this defended settlement type is rare [S2]; such settlements were occupied by small communities [S3]; they were perhaps occupied by a single family group [S3]; the site has been partially excavated [S6]; the excavation found hammer stones [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-065 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shipton Hill Settlement is an archaeological site, a defended settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; It dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; It is in Dorset [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; A defended settlement is a rare monument type [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; Such settlements were occupied by small communities [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; The occupying group was perhaps a single family group [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; The site has been partly excavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; Hammer stones were found in the excavation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]; Such settlements are smaller than hillforts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipton_Hill_Settlement]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b3528337-0230-4210-a01c-dadd353b879e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Coney''s Castle, an Iron Age hillfort in Dorset, rides a narrow ridge reaching 210 m, a small lane cutting through it. Its name, from Old English for rabbit, hints at a medieval warren.', 'Sited on a narrow north-south ridge at 210 meters, this Dorset fort uses steep natural slopes to the west and an artificial rampart and ditch to the east. The Wessex Ridgeway trail passes through it.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b3528337-0230-4210-a01c-dadd353b879e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Sited on a narrow north-south ridge at 210 meters, this Dorset fort uses steep natural slopes to the west and an artificial rampart and ditch to the east. The Wessex Ridgeway trail passes through it.'' restored on Coney''s Castle', '1d50438744a13dce990a16bd10828481b3fea3d74a38b2a2c0c513d5b72c3597|13f3e060c0359f56d7a02a0d49a2c5c74a0b4fb48986aa3a471b6478931e56f2|13f3e060c0359f56d7a02a0d49a2c5c74a0b4fb48986aa3a471b6478931e56f2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Coney''s Castle, an Iron Age hillfort in Dorset, rides a narrow ridge reaching 210 m, a small lane cutting through it. Its name, from Old English for rabbit, hints at a medieval warren."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Coney''s Castle is an Iron Age hillfort [S1]; it is in Dorset [S1]; it stands on a narrow ridge [S3]; the ridge reaches 210 m [S3]; a small lane cuts through it [S4]; the name comes from Old English for rabbit [S2]; the name hints (suggests) medieval use as a warren [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-065 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Coney''s Castle is a hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; Coney''s Castle is Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; Coney''s Castle is in Dorset [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; The fort lies on a narrow ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; The ridge reaches 210 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; A small lane cuts through the hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; The name Coney is from the Old English for rabbit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]; The name hints at a medieval warren [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coney%27s_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b367310b-a211-40b3-b376-80e9da0fdd32'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the island of Orust, four raised stone slabs hold up a fifth as a roof: the Haga Dolmen, small in size yet with the province''s largest inside chamber, roughly dated to about 3400 BCE.', 'A compact Neolithic dolmen from 3400 BC with four uprights, a roof slab, and a threshold stone. Small in size but perfectly preserved on its island setting.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b367310b-a211-40b3-b376-80e9da0fdd32:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A compact Neolithic dolmen from 3400 BC with four uprights, a roof slab, and a threshold stone. Small in size but perfectly preserved on its island setting.'' restored on Haga Dolmen', '462605e4ff9cd5482b81e57cd30e5d2677f0f62b2c96bd268515a67556856b56|76aca8b7c532704eaa4476de7cf996d96c57cadd4c75400e395dd06c18890d0d|76aca8b7c532704eaa4476de7cf996d96c57cadd4c75400e395dd06c18890d0d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the island of Orust, four raised stone slabs hold up a fifth as a roof: the Haga Dolmen, small in size yet with the province''s largest inside chamber, roughly dated to about 3400 BCE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on the island of Orust [S2]; four raised stone slabs hold up a fifth as a roof [S3]; the site is the Haga Dolmen [S1]; it is small in size [S5]; it has the province''s largest inside chamber [S5]; it is roughly dated to about 3400 BCE [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-065 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Haga Dolmen is a dolmen (megalithic grave) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haga_dolmen]; It is on the island of Orust [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haga_dolmen]; Four raised stone slabs form the chamber [SUPPORTED: https://orust.se/uppleva-och-gora/kulturhistoria-kulturarv/megalitgravar/hagadosen]; A fifth slab is the roof [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haga_dolmen]; The dolmen is small in size [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haga_dolmen]; It has the province''s largest inside chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haga_dolmen]; It is roughly dated to about 3400 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haga_dolmen]; It was probably built around 3400 BC (Swedish local authority) [SUPPORTED: https://orust.se/uppleva-och-gora/kulturhistoria-kulturarv/megalitgravar/hagadosen]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b5899722-5118-41e0-8324-5da705ace4a2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'South of Portbury near Bristol lies Conygar Hillfort, a small multivallate Iron Age fort of triangular shape, where the remains of a bank 3 feet high mark the south-western side.', 'Conygar Hillfort is a small multivallate Iron Age hill fort in the North Somerset district of Somerset.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b5899722-5118-41e0-8324-5da705ace4a2:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Conygar Hillfort is a small multivallate Iron Age hill fort in the North Somerset district of Somerset.'' restored on Conygar Hillfort', '2159446bea70bd86fe2bd847156e38b73366c81b6df7e18faed0c841eb7b59f8|fccde166a74eba9f34afdaee1538af5270c46208d3f8db78aab5563a128ba781|fccde166a74eba9f34afdaee1538af5270c46208d3f8db78aab5563a128ba781', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "South of Portbury near Bristol lies Conygar Hillfort, a small multivallate Iron Age fort of triangular shape, where the remains of a bank 3 feet high mark the south-western side."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies south of Portbury [S3]; Portbury is near Bristol [S3]; the site is Conygar Hillfort [S1]; it is small [S1]; it is multivallate [S1]; it is an Iron Age fort [S1]; it is triangular in shape [S4]; the remains of a bank 3 feet high are on the south-western side [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-066 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Conygar Hillfort is a fort (a hill fort) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; It is in England (Somerset) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; It lies near Bristol [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; It lies south of Portbury [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; The fort is small [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; The fort is multivallate [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; The fort is Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; The fort is triangular in shape [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; The remains of a bank 3 feet high survive [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]; The bank remains mark the south-western side [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conygar_Hillfort]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b596bc7e-15ff-4dfd-b052-710626210123'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a small hill near Rovinj, Monkodonja was ringed by three concentric dry stone walls of Cyclopean masonry reminiscent of Mycenean work, and Bronze Age cist graves lay in its western gate.', 'Monkodonja lies on a small hill, 81 m above sea level, and forms an irregular ellipse stretching east–west that is 250 m long and 160 m wide.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b596bc7e-15ff-4dfd-b052-710626210123:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Monkodonja lies on a small hill, 81 m above sea level, and forms an irregular ellipse stretching east–west that is 250 m long and 160 m wide.'' restored on Monkodonja', 'f8f90b99dc19869e7323aa0dee7ca318540f888a925eeb9537cb639bd1fb50db|aac8815f2689133bc4c88bcf672e5bf81086d2bdb40beaf7537da0ad6b470a41|aac8815f2689133bc4c88bcf672e5bf81086d2bdb40beaf7537da0ad6b470a41', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a small hill near Rovinj, Monkodonja was ringed by three concentric dry stone walls of Cyclopean masonry reminiscent of Mycenean work, and Bronze Age cist graves lay in its western gate."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on a small hill [S3]; it is near Rovinj [S1]; the site is Monkodonja [S1]; it was surrounded by three concentric walls [S4]; the walls are dry stone [S5]; the walls are of Cyclopean masonry [S5]; the masonry is reminiscent of Mycenean architecture [S5]; Bronze Age cist graves were found in the western gate [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-066 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monkodonja is a hillfort (fortified settlement) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; It is near Rovinj [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; It is in Croatia (Istria) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; It stands on a small hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; It was ringed (surrounded) by three walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; The three walls are concentric [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; The walls are dry stone constructions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; The walls use Cyclopean masonry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; The masonry is reminiscent of Mycenean work [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; Cist graves lay in its western gate [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]; The cist graves are Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkodonja]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b60c114a-7ee2-4f67-b3ff-a3d3d3e55d15'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Billingsgate Roman House and Baths in Londinium, a bath with hypocausts survives best. Digs found several hundred late 4th century coins and, in fallen roof debris, an Anglo-Saxon brooch.', 'A Roman house in Londinium with well-preserved underfloor heating intact beneath the streets. The east wing had hypocausts, and the building sat at the Roman waterfront.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b60c114a-7ee2-4f67-b3ff-a3d3d3e55d15:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A Roman house in Londinium with well-preserved underfloor heating intact beneath the streets. The east wing had hypocausts, and the building sat at the Roman waterfront.'' restored on Billingsgate Roman House and Baths', '40804578d198c7e92e5ebcaa06380097296990413781ab6bc47ac5fc3649a9c4|189f222a391f19195b79a614b57f9a91fd5c60a02a294ad9a526a88589b36e3d|189f222a391f19195b79a614b57f9a91fd5c60a02a294ad9a526a88589b36e3d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Billingsgate Roman House and Baths in Londinium, a bath with hypocausts survives best. Digs found several hundred late 4th century coins and, in fallen roof debris, an Anglo-Saxon brooch."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Billingsgate Roman House and Baths [S1]; it is in Londinium [S1]; a bath with hypocausts is the best preserved part [S2]; excavations found several hundred coins [S7]; the coins date to the late 4th century [S7]; an Anglo-Saxon brooch was found [S8]; the brooch was found in fallen material from the roof [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-066 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Billingsgate Roman House and Baths is an archaeological site of a Roman house and baths [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; It is in Londinium (Roman London) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; A bath with hypocausts survives (is preserved) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; The bath with hypocausts is the best preserved part [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; Excavations found several hundred coins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; The coins date to the late 4th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; An Anglo-Saxon brooch was found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]; The brooch lay in fallen roof debris [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billingsgate_Roman_House_and_Baths]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b672af27-ee37-4815-bd7c-f1a5e09f2413'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Building an underpass in Plovdiv uncovered the Domus Eirene, a Roman peristyle house whose lavish mosaic floors show endless knots, flowers and inscriptions welcoming visitors.', 'A 3rd-century AD Roman house with lavish mosaic floors, built after Goths wrecked the city in 250 AD. The mosaics were a statement of defiant reconstruction.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b672af27-ee37-4815-bd7c-f1a5e09f2413:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A 3rd-century AD Roman house with lavish mosaic floors, built after Goths wrecked the city in 250 AD. The mosaics were a statement of defiant reconstruction.'' restored on Domus Eirene', 'd9fa331830d3be806e4ad525218debc682df518a11a3a0c799a285ad06378a23|3eb13938d6b455e5ba8a14658878c644d42ef0062375ca5378416831cc1c1cd2|3eb13938d6b455e5ba8a14658878c644d42ef0062375ca5378416831cc1c1cd2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Building an underpass in Plovdiv uncovered the Domus Eirene, a Roman peristyle house whose lavish mosaic floors show endless knots, flowers and inscriptions welcoming visitors."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the house was uncovered during the building of an underpass [S7]; the underpass is in Plovdiv [S7]; the site is the Domus Eirene [S1]; it is a Roman peristyle house [S1]; its mosaic floors are lavish [S1, S4]; the mosaics show endless knots [S4]; the mosaics show flowers [S4]; the mosaics show inscriptions welcoming visitors [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-066 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Domus Eirene is a Roman house (a domus) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; It is a peristyle house [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; It has lavish mosaic floors [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; It is in Plovdiv (Bulgaria) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; It was uncovered during construction of an underpass [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; The mosaics show endless knots [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; The mosaics show flowers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]; The mosaics include inscriptions welcoming visitors [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domus_Eirene]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b6aab229-8793-40ad-a697-c8dbf6e331bf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Within the ramparts of Grovely Castle, an Iron Age hill fort in Wiltshire, excavations uncovered five human skeletons, while aerial photographs reveal a circular enclosure in its interior.', 'Ramparts still standing 3.2 metres high ring this univallate hillfort in Wiltshire. Excavations revealed five human skeletons buried within the earthwork walls themselves.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b6aab229-8793-40ad-a697-c8dbf6e331bf:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Ramparts still standing 3.2 metres high ring this univallate hillfort in Wiltshire. Excavations revealed five human skeletons buried within the earthwork walls themselves.'' restored on Grovely Castle', 'a1c6df0280ad9d113e32525089ad0c2d78888ce714036c2bc5364c1effaae13b|6f9d3447df21efd58a3893e228fde074efa207d50e179efa4f018497b8a16834|6f9d3447df21efd58a3893e228fde074efa207d50e179efa4f018497b8a16834', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-022", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Within the ramparts of Grovely Castle, an Iron Age hill fort in Wiltshire, excavations uncovered five human skeletons, while aerial photographs reveal a circular enclosure in its interior."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-022 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Grovely Castle [S1]; it is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; it is in Wiltshire [S1]; excavations uncovered five human skeletons [S3]; the skeletons were within the ramparts [S3]; aerial photographs reveal a circular enclosure [S5]; the enclosure is in the interior of the hillfort [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-066 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Grovely Castle is a hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]; It is Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]; It is in Wiltshire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]; Excavations uncovered five human skeletons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]; The skeletons lay within the ramparts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]; Aerial photographs reveal a circular enclosure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]; The circular enclosure is in the interior of the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grovely_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b774f9cc-ea01-4658-b12c-c66add4453d1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Below the crest of Copehill Down lies White Barrow, a Neolithic long barrow never fully excavated. A 1997 survey found evidence of a possible burial chamber inside, and an arc of post pits.', 'At 77.5 meters long on Salisbury Plain near Tilshead, this Neolithic long barrow dates to 4000–3500 BC. Never fully excavated, it sits within the broader Stonehenge ritual landscape.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b774f9cc-ea01-4658-b12c-c66add4453d1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''At 77.5 meters long on Salisbury Plain near Tilshead, this Neolithic long barrow dates to 4000–3500 BC. Never fully excavated, it sits within the broader Stonehenge ritual landscape.'' restored on White Barrow', '51da852bd0d3d36e8899e202f2de804eaeb1d85be493e95201ad5c56f016ab56|ac5a5c3e605a76be42c4e77474b32af1d125572dabf64dc15b8c2e99c78aeca9|ac5a5c3e605a76be42c4e77474b32af1d125572dabf64dc15b8c2e99c78aeca9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Below the crest of Copehill Down lies White Barrow, a Neolithic long barrow never fully excavated. A 1997 survey found evidence of a possible burial chamber inside, and an arc of post pits."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is White Barrow [S1]; it lies just below the crest of Copehill Down [S1]; it is a Neolithic long barrow [S1]; it has never been fully excavated [S4]; a survey in 1997 found evidence of a possible burial chamber inside the barrow [S5]; the survey also found evidence of an arc of post pits [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "White Barrow is a long barrow (a tomb monument) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]; White Barrow is Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]; White Barrow lies below the crest of Copehill Down (Salisbury Plain, Wiltshire, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]; The barrow has never been fully excavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]; A survey in 1997 examined the barrow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]; The 1997 survey found evidence of a possible burial chamber inside the barrow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]; The 1997 survey also found an arc of post pits [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b8583e63-17c5-48b0-a4d1-018acaf608c1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Iron Age Meare Lake Village was raised over a morass on timber, brushwood and clay. Its ruins gave up red deer antler, a spiral finger ring of bronze and a decorated amber bead.', '300 BC–100 AD. Two Iron Age villages once floated within Meare Pool on the Somerset Levels — a bog lake now drained. Each village mirrors the nearby Glastonbury Lake Village in form and era.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:b8583e63-17c5-48b0-a4d1-018acaf608c1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''300 BC–100 AD. Two Iron Age villages once floated within Meare Pool on the Somerset Levels — a bog lake now drained. Each village mirrors the nearby Glastonbury Lake Village in form and era.'' restored on Meare Lake Village', '49424301510435ced5fd924469ca15b3233e4351920b01ff161e318a5ad286ff|73408aaf783fca599927dc6b5db6844bcddf53ab58bcd6bfd6f9628ba8c1a465|73408aaf783fca599927dc6b5db6844bcddf53ab58bcd6bfd6f9628ba8c1a465', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Iron Age Meare Lake Village was raised over a morass on timber, brushwood and clay. Its ruins gave up red deer antler, a spiral finger ring of bronze and a decorated amber bead."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Meare Lake Village [S1]; it is an Iron Age settlement [S1]; it was built over a morass [S3]; it stood on an artificial foundation of timber [S3]; the foundation was filled with brushwood and clay [S3]; archaeologists found red deer antler (cut pieces) there [S8]; archaeologists found a spiral finger ring made of bronze there [S8]; archaeologists found a decorated amber bead there [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Meare Lake Village is a settlement (a village on the Somerset Levels at Meare, Somerset, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; The village dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; The village was raised over a morass [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; Its artificial foundation was made of timber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; Its foundation was filled with brushwood [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; Its foundation contained clay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; Excavation of its ruins yielded red deer antler (cut pieces) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; Excavation of its ruins yielded a spiral finger ring of bronze [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]; Excavation of its ruins yielded a decorated amber bead [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meare_Lake_Village]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ba0866bd-4186-4722-8e4d-ea67e228062b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Copper was mined at Apliki from the late 14th Century BC. On its rocky terraces stood a smelter site, and four houses gave up an ivory cylinder, a steatite seal and a gold earring.', 'Four houses were excavated, with rich findings, including an ivory cylinder, a cylinder seal of steatite and a gold earring.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ba0866bd-4186-4722-8e4d-ea67e228062b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Four houses were excavated, with rich findings, including an ivory cylinder, a cylinder seal of steatite and a gold earring.'' restored on Apliki', '96c2ec2809558fcd35b54284dca82a587b07a8d757e96cc4c33a6566946f11bf|c36996514f70a82dc5143a9018b2e449e98ad2714b59771a8c92a5aa7013abe5|c36996514f70a82dc5143a9018b2e449e98ad2714b59771a8c92a5aa7013abe5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Copper was mined at Apliki from the late 14th Century BC. On its rocky terraces stood a smelter site, and four houses gave up an ivory cylinder, a steatite seal and a gold earring."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Apliki [S1]; copper was mined at Apliki [S1]; the mining dates from the late 14th Century BC [S1]; the site has rocky terraces [S2]; a smelter site stood on the terraces [S2]; four houses were excavated [S3]; the houses yielded an ivory cylinder [S3]; the houses yielded a steatite (cylinder) seal [S3]; the houses yielded a gold earring [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Apliki is a copper mining and smelting site in Cyprus (Nicosia District) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; Copper was mined at Apliki [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; Copper mining at Apliki began in the late 14th Century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; Apliki had a smelter site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; The smelter site stood on rocky terraces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; Four houses were excavated and gave up rich finds [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; The houses gave up an ivory cylinder [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; The houses gave up a steatite seal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]; The houses gave up a gold earring [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apliki]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bb263810-ca13-4ab1-b97d-d5ce2b2d0ab3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'They call it the Lost City of Huayuri: ruins stretching about 500 metres along a ravine between two ridges, where rainfall harvesting began between 1260 and 1290 CE.', 'Pre-Columbian city flourishing 1150–1450 AD in the Ica region at 500 metres elevation. Its growth coincided with a wetter climatic phase — when the rains stopped, so did the city.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bb263810-ca13-4ab1-b97d-d5ce2b2d0ab3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Pre-Columbian city flourishing 1150–1450 AD in the Ica region at 500 metres elevation. Its growth coincided with a wetter climatic phase — when the rains stopped, so did the city.'' restored on Huayuri', 'e67fac08671aaddeda75be660fd3ca5f00af13154f6de27f3af9e222e51d5509|6598ae01ae7b24a1b00e2afec7c82b52e9bc3752fd893c90965849a2c030e83f|6598ae01ae7b24a1b00e2afec7c82b52e9bc3752fd893c90965849a2c030e83f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "They call it the Lost City of Huayuri: ruins stretching about 500 metres along a ravine between two ridges, where rainfall harvesting began between 1260 and 1290 CE."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is called the Lost City of Huayuri (as well as Huayuri) [S1]; its ruins extend about 500 metres [S3]; the ruins lie along a ravine [S3]; the ravine lies between two ridges [S3]; rainfall harvesting began there between 1260 and 1290 CE [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huayuri is a large pre-Columbian archaeological site (a town or city of stone houses) in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayuri]; Huayuri is called the Lost City of Huayuri [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayuri]; The ruins stretch about 500 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayuri]; The ruins lie along a ravine between two ridges [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayuri]; Rainfall harvesting began at Huayuri between 1260 and 1290 CE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayuri]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bbe632de-a422-458f-8379-bef7ed34d4ef'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Bulls-eye shaped ceremonial buildings rise on the hill slopes of Los Guachimontones, in number and size by far the largest of several dozen Teuchitlán Culture sites in the Tequila Valleys.', 'A ceremonial center built from 300 BC with a unique design: stepped circular pyramids surrounded by ring-shaped platforms. This concentric layout is unlike any other Mesoamerican architecture.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bbe632de-a422-458f-8379-bef7ed34d4ef:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A ceremonial center built from 300 BC with a unique design: stepped circular pyramids surrounded by ring-shaped platforms. This concentric layout is unlike any other Mesoamerican architecture.'' restored on Los Guachimontones', 'dff540bc12f4c006a49ec7a3ed87a26801510adfa50a98d1f105d25c2f37c2e1|400634928c18c86c51b92a85418463074d20be48cd9e5343f6188a136cede7b5|400634928c18c86c51b92a85418463074d20be48cd9e5343f6188a136cede7b5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bulls-eye shaped ceremonial buildings rise on the hill slopes of Los Guachimontones, in number and size by far the largest of several dozen Teuchitlán Culture sites in the Tequila Valleys."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Los Guachimontones [S1]; it has ceremonial buildings [S2]; the buildings are bulls-eye shaped [S3]; the buildings rise on the hill slopes of the site [S7]; it is one of several dozen Teuchitlan Culture sites [S2]; the sites lie in the Tequila Valleys [S2]; it is by far the largest of them in the number and size of its ceremonial buildings [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-067 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Los Guachimontones is a pre-Columbian archaeological site with ceremonial buildings, in Jalisco, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; Los Guachimontones has ceremonial buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; The ceremonial buildings are bulls-eye shaped [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; The ceremonial buildings rise on the hill slopes of Los Guachimontones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; Los Guachimontones is one of several dozen Teuchitlán Culture sites [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; The Teuchitlán Culture sites lie in the Tequila Valleys [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; Los Guachimontones is by far the largest of those sites in the number of its ceremonial buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]; Los Guachimontones is by far the largest of those sites in the size of its ceremonial buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Guachimontones]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bc28c81f-db22-46a5-bc4e-18cb8c2ecbec'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Deep in the jungles of Campeche, Structure 2 of Calakmul rises over 45 metres. This Maya city rivalled Tikal throughout the Classic period and lost to it in a decisive battle in AD 695.', 'Vast Maya lowland city that rivalled Tikal as a regional superpower during the Classic period. Soft limestone construction spans a site with evidence from the Preclassic through Postclassic periods.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bc28c81f-db22-46a5-bc4e-18cb8c2ecbec:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Vast Maya lowland city that rivalled Tikal as a regional superpower during the Classic period. Soft limestone construction spans a site with evidence from the Preclassic through Postclassic periods.'' restored on Calakmul', '7534b677550778407d54ea84baf429024840693e7711af4596860b2e9344ba95|d08f1359d7e48f02ca39e50b553a6ac27eb04bfcdf62c72829408e223988ae28|d08f1359d7e48f02ca39e50b553a6ac27eb04bfcdf62c72829408e223988ae28', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Deep in the jungles of Campeche, Structure 2 of Calakmul rises over 45 metres. This Maya city rivalled Tikal throughout the Classic period and lost to it in a decisive battle in AD 695."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Calakmul [S1]; it lies deep in the jungles [S1]; it lies in Campeche [S1]; Structure 2 rises over 45 metres [S4]; Calakmul is a Maya city [S1, S2]; it rivalled Tikal throughout the Classic period [S5]; it lost to Tikal in a decisive battle [S7]; the decisive battle took place in AD 695 [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Calakmul is a Maya archaeological site, a Maya city (the central claim: the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Calakmul lies in Campeche, Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Calakmul lies deep in the jungles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Calakmul was a Maya city (one of the largest and most powerful) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Structure 2 is a structure (a massive pyramid temple) of Calakmul [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Structure 2 rises over 45 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Calakmul rivalled Tikal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; The rivalry with Tikal lasted throughout the Classic period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; Calakmul lost to Tikal in a decisive battle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]; The decisive battle between Tikal and Calakmul took place in AD 695 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calakmul]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bcf13ede-0f30-415a-85ea-59bfd6d4cce4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Named after Mars, Roman god of war, the garrison of Castra Martis guarded the road through the Vrashka Chuka pass. In 408 the Huns under Uldin took it, apparently by treachery.', 'A Roman fortress named after Mars, the god of war, guarding a mountain pass. It grew important enough to become a bishopric in the province of Dacia Ripensis.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bcf13ede-0f30-415a-85ea-59bfd6d4cce4:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A Roman fortress named after Mars, the god of war, guarding a mountain pass. It grew important enough to become a bishopric in the province of Dacia Ripensis.'' restored on Castra Martis', 'eb47fb5a322f9fa8350101aa05d94b5d8d1b5ab8754fb186868e6f68a88d8ae0|20787234afe20cdb83f3cb75fceea8603e5491642dcc2c638e6d6fc1e60c7789|20787234afe20cdb83f3cb75fceea8603e5491642dcc2c638e6d6fc1e60c7789', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Named after Mars, Roman god of war, the garrison of Castra Martis guarded the road through the Vrashka Chuka pass. In 408 the Huns under Uldin took it, apparently by treachery."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Castra Martis [S1, S2]; it was named after Mars [S2]; Mars is the Roman god of war [S2]; Castra Martis was a garrison [S1]; it guarded the road through the Vrashka Chuka pass [S2]; in 408 the Huns took the site [S5]; the Huns were under Uldin [S5]; the taking was apparently by treachery [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Castra Martis was a Roman fortified garrison (castra), the central claim: the kind of place [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; Castra Martis lay at the modern site of Kula in Vidin Province, northwestern Bulgaria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; Castra Martis was named after Mars [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; Mars is the Roman god of war [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; The garrison guarded the road through the Vrashka Chuka pass [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; In 408 (the date) Castra Martis was taken [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; The Huns under Uldin took Castra Martis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castra_martis]; Uldin took it apparently by treachery [SUPPORTED: https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/26029.htm]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bd3f6a4e-0452-448a-8674-b8a87c0e63e8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the limestone of Magura Cave, prehistoric painters left hunting scenes, religious ceremonies and deities, among them the Fertility Dance and the Hunting Ceremony.', 'A cave with prehistoric paintings spanning thousands of years, drawn in bat guano. The images include a solar calendar, dancing figures, and hunting scenes of striking expressiveness.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bd3f6a4e-0452-448a-8674-b8a87c0e63e8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A cave with prehistoric paintings spanning thousands of years, drawn in bat guano. The images include a solar calendar, dancing figures, and hunting scenes of striking expressiveness.'' restored on Magura Cave', '4ae0356eb03bb04fce508b9b8d56885da1e33ef8ad92afe6765ea5d13377b120|4059c067dabbf48c614148fd8ab1e6be38aae81d949e38bacf4350ed29e17e99|4059c067dabbf48c614148fd8ab1e6be38aae81d949e38bacf4350ed29e17e99', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the limestone of Magura Cave, prehistoric painters left hunting scenes, religious ceremonies and deities, among them the Fertility Dance and the Hunting Ceremony."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Magura Cave [S1]; the cave lies in limestone [S4]; prehistoric people painted in the cave [S2]; the paintings show hunting scenes [S7]; the paintings show religious ceremonies [S7]; the paintings show deities [S7]; the Fertility Dance and the Hunting Ceremony are among the paintings [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Magura Cave is a cave (in Bulgaria), the central claim: the kind of place [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; Magura Cave is formed in limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The cave has paintings on its walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The paintings are prehistoric [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The paintings show hunting scenes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The paintings show religious ceremonies [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The paintings show deities [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The Fertility Dance is among the paintings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]; The Hunting Ceremony is among the paintings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magura_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bdbf1e68-32c8-4571-90c0-ef154d458704'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Franklin River in Tasmania lies Kutikina Cave, the most southerly human occupation in the world during the last ice age, with evidence of wallaby hunting on open tundra.', 'Kutikina Cave is a rock shelter located on the Franklin River in the South West Wilderness.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bdbf1e68-32c8-4571-90c0-ef154d458704:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Kutikina Cave is a rock shelter located on the Franklin River in the South West Wilderness.'' restored on Kutikina Cave', '61afa09d29d70142f1d936e30ccd8e60976232ec69f2fc0c2629c406d1e2f790|acadb2a1542d0d9b7f93bbf04930ec96cf12b2cc276a72ab37fa3be2078fe820|acadb2a1542d0d9b7f93bbf04930ec96cf12b2cc276a72ab37fa3be2078fe820', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Franklin River in Tasmania lies Kutikina Cave, the most southerly human occupation in the world during the last ice age, with evidence of wallaby hunting on open tundra."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Kutikina Cave [S1]; it lies on the Franklin River [S1]; it lies in Tasmania [S1]; it was the most southerly human occupation in the world during the last ice age [S4]; there is evidence of wallaby hunting [S4]; the landscape was open tundra at that time [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kutikina Cave is a cave (rock shelter) with archaeological deposits of human occupation, the central claim: the kind of place [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]; Kutikina Cave lies on the Franklin River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]; The Franklin River and the cave are in Tasmania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]; Kutikina Cave was the most southerly human occupation in the world [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]; That was during the last ice age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]; The cave has evidence of wallaby hunting [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]; The wallaby hunting took place on open tundra [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutikina_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('be81c1a6-cd42-4fea-8bb9-323bf0a1c586'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Three of the six Baalbek Stones form the Trilithon in a temple podium wall: Roman blocks each 19 metres long and around 750–800 tonnes, a gigantism unparalleled in antiquity.', 'Six massive Roman-era stone blocks, the largest weighing over 1,200 tonnes — the heaviest cut stones known. They were quarried but never moved to their intended position.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:be81c1a6-cd42-4fea-8bb9-323bf0a1c586:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Six massive Roman-era stone blocks, the largest weighing over 1,200 tonnes — the heaviest cut stones known. They were quarried but never moved to their intended position.'' restored on Baalbek Stones', '2de04532b1b6e44da698972eb8f5a2b4fd5b3153d95d6107bb8ea7d1e44e7717|d562038825b3a4dddd21f928f52b171c311298668458987062b3979216856118|d562038825b3a4dddd21f928f52b171c311298668458987062b3979216856118', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Three of the six Baalbek Stones form the Trilithon in a temple podium wall: Roman blocks each 19 metres long and around 750–800 tonnes, a gigantism unparalleled in antiquity."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is the Baalbek Stones [S1]; there are six Baalbek Stones [S1]; three of them form the Trilithon [S2]; the Trilithon is part of a podium wall of the temple complex [S2]; the blocks are Roman [S1]; each block is 19 metres long [S3]; each block weighs around 750-800 tonnes [S3]; the gigantism of the stones is unparalleled in antiquity [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-068 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Baalbek Stones are massive Roman worked stone blocks at Baalbek, Lebanon, the central claim: the kind of place [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; There are six Baalbek Stones and they are Roman [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; Three of the six stones form the Trilithon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; The Trilithon is part of a podium wall of a temple (Temple of Jupiter Baal) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; The Trilithon blocks are Roman work [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; Each Trilithon block is 19 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; Each Trilithon block weighs around 750–800 tonnes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]; The stones show a gigantism unparalleled in antiquity [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baalbek_Stones]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('bf43132d-1cbc-4dd2-9cf9-914a917e78d9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'High on a mountain named Coyor, about 2,970 metres up and east of Lake San Nicolas, lies Collor, an archaeological site whose name may come from the Quechua word for star.', 'The site lies at a height of about 2,970 metres on a mountain named Coyor, east of Lake San Nicolas .', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:bf43132d-1cbc-4dd2-9cf9-914a917e78d9:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The site lies at a height of about 2,970 metres on a mountain named Coyor, east of Lake San Nicolas .'' restored on Collor', '041128d65bb7ce161511b78b844b40307cc030136c092a626454bfdd032bfc89|fde784a264010af0b5ad12caa57b4d6c17abe2e92a6cbd587c0ed4ba079ed8dd|fde784a264010af0b5ad12caa57b4d6c17abe2e92a6cbd587c0ed4ba079ed8dd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "High on a mountain named Coyor, about 2,970 metres up and east of Lake San Nicolas, lies Collor, an archaeological site whose name may come from the Quechua word for star."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Collor [S1]; it lies on a mountain named Coyor [S3]; it lies at about 2,970 metres [S3]; it lies east of Lake San Nicolas [S3]; it is an archaeological site [S1]; its name may (possibly) come from the Quechua word for star [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Collor is an archaeological site (central claim: the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]; The site lies on a mountain named Coyor [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]; The site lies about 2,970 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]; The site is high on the mountain (the text says ''High on a mountain'') [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]; The mountain is east of Lake San Nicolas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]; The name Collor may come from a Quechua word (possibly, not certainly) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]; The Quechua word (quyllur) means star [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collor]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c070b6b0-3fd2-4d6c-acbe-105539188253'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Around 480,000 years ago, early humans hunted and butchered here: Eartham Pit, Boxgrove, one of the oldest sites in Europe with direct evidence of both, now some 10 kilometres inland.', 'Homo heidelbergensis fossils 500,000 years old make this the oldest human-remains site in the country. Butchered animal bones here are among the earliest proof of hunting by early humans in Europe.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c070b6b0-3fd2-4d6c-acbe-105539188253:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Homo heidelbergensis fossils 500,000 years old make this the oldest human-remains site in the country. Butchered animal bones here are among the earliest proof of hunting by early humans in Europe.'' restored on Eartham Pit, Boxgrove', 'f3cdd83d6e9f27a94a87b295686346d4cb99c55488cd4fc16b947165b35c0fe4|8a16a72cf7a8d1de75e9c7d7efd254fba720b461f13d734083f026dbeae3b706|8a16a72cf7a8d1de75e9c7d7efd254fba720b461f13d734083f026dbeae3b706', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Around 480,000 years ago, early humans hunted and butchered here: Eartham Pit, Boxgrove, one of the oldest sites in Europe with direct evidence of both, now some 10 kilometres inland."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Eartham Pit, Boxgrove [S1]; the site dates to around 480,000 years ago [S1]; early humans hunted at the site [S2]; early humans butchered animals at the site [S2]; it is one of the oldest sites in Europe with direct evidence of hunting and butchering [S2]; the site is now some 10 kilometres inland [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Eartham Pit, Boxgrove is a Lower Palaeolithic archaeological site (central claim: the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove_Palaeolithic_site]; The site is at Boxgrove and the quarry is known as Eartham Pit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove]; The site dates to around 480,000 years ago [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove_Palaeolithic_site]; Early humans hunted at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove_Palaeolithic_site]; Early humans butchered animals at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove_Palaeolithic_site]; It is one of the oldest sites in Europe with direct evidence of both hunting and butchering [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove_Palaeolithic_site]; The site is now some 10 kilometres inland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boxgrove_Palaeolithic_site]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c08d8ce8-6103-4f32-bb80-9e59c0a97a17'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Paleolithic people once lived in Buzeyir Cave, where digs unearthed items of stone and sharpened animal bones. Found in 1985, it has yielded around 74 artefacts.', 'The cave was discovered in 1985, during an archaeological expedition in the Lankaran, Lerik, and Masally Rayons under the guidance of Asadulla Jafarov.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c08d8ce8-6103-4f32-bb80-9e59c0a97a17:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The cave was discovered in 1985, during an archaeological expedition in the Lankaran, Lerik, and Masally Rayons under the guidance of Asadulla Jafarov.'' restored on Buzeyir Cave', '8c8c0f6600a6084704aa5587424b7ec66a7bcd74eb4f35f7876ee5bc5d77e0e7|acebf2900107b2296ef59346e619cba6d45f27f8e720b1646c05fec0d2c394bb|acebf2900107b2296ef59346e619cba6d45f27f8e720b1646c05fec0d2c394bb', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Paleolithic people once lived in Buzeyir Cave, where digs unearthed items of stone and sharpened animal bones. Found in 1985, it has yielded around 74 artefacts."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Buzeyir Cave [S1]; Paleolithic people lived in the cave [S1]; excavations unearthed items of stone [S3]; excavations unearthed sharpened animal bones [S3]; the cave was found in 1985 [S2]; around 74 artefacts have been found in it [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Buzeyir Cave is an archaeological site, a cave (central claim: the kind of place) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]; Paleolithic people once lived in Buzeyir Cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]; Excavations unearthed items of stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]; Excavations unearthed sharpened animal bones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]; The cave was found in 1985 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]; The cave has yielded around 74 artefacts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]; The cave is in Azerbaijan (country given in the header) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzeyir_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c1448f84-e07a-436a-8110-087f293a6564'::uuid, 'card_description', 'West-southwest of Hanover, right on the city boundary, the Benther Berg ridge climbs to 173.3 m above sea level. On it lie protohistorical tumuli from the Bronze Age.', 'Bronze Age tumuli dot a 3.5-kilometre ridge rising to 173 metres above the Hanover plain. The hill''s name comes from the village of Benthe below — the mounds remain its most ancient feature.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c1448f84-e07a-436a-8110-087f293a6564:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description "Bronze Age tumuli dot a 3.5-kilometre ridge rising to 173 metres above the Hanover plain. The hill''s name comes from the village of Benthe below — the mounds remain its most ancient feature." restored on Benther Berg', 'baea839b213f7fbb7f7ea38d2b7bc5feeb14af136127aca4c5b30b7aaf318299|f7b4a43e7a77cae8330ea774eccf339ec77c0f86155f206f21690b873671be24|f7b4a43e7a77cae8330ea774eccf339ec77c0f86155f206f21690b873671be24', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "West-southwest of Hanover, right on the city boundary, the Benther Berg ridge climbs to 173.3 m above sea level. On it lie protohistorical tumuli from the Bronze Age."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is the Benther Berg [S1]; it is a ridge [S1]; it lies west-southwest of Hanover [S3]; it borders directly on the city boundary [S3]; it reaches up to 173.3 m above sea level [S1]; protohistorical tumuli lie on it [S4]; the tumuli date from the Bronze Age [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Benther Berg is a ridge (central claim: the kind of place, with Bronze Age tumuli on it) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; The ridge climbs to 173.3 m above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; The ridge lies west-southwest of Hanover [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; The ridge lies right on the Hanover city boundary [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; There are tumuli on the Benther Berg [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; The tumuli are protohistorical [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; The tumuli date from the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]; The ridge is in the Hanover area (near Benthe in Hanover Region, Germany) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benther_Berg]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c2cd2a6a-8f1d-4fce-9645-234908ef7d37'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Hodson Stone Circle once stood in Wiltshire; in the 1890s A. D. Passmore recorded eight stones and a possible avenue. Some of the village''s building sarsens were once part of the ring.', 'Stone circle in Wiltshire discovered by antiquarian A. D. Passmore in the 1890s. He noted eight stones with a possible avenue emerging — documented fully only in his unpublished notebooks.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c2cd2a6a-8f1d-4fce-9645-234908ef7d37:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Stone circle in Wiltshire discovered by antiquarian A. D. Passmore in the 1890s. He noted eight stones with a possible avenue emerging — documented fully only in his unpublished notebooks.'' restored on Hodson Stone Circle', 'd3911a78dd580663666059a13366e1651b8a78c8d6e404156557efd59a62bba8|b37a98596339befdca7742df8b71938f87a279b76a8a64919e1f9a7b11c665e4|b37a98596339befdca7742df8b71938f87a279b76a8a64919e1f9a7b11c665e4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-023", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hodson Stone Circle once stood in Wiltshire; in the 1890s A. D. Passmore recorded eight stones and a possible avenue. Some of the village''s building sarsens were once part of the ring."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-023 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Hodson Stone Circle [S1]; it once stood in Wiltshire [S1]; A. D. Passmore recorded it in the 1890s [S3]; he recorded eight stones [S5]; he recorded a possible avenue [S5]; some of the sarsens used as building material in the village were once part of the circle [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-069 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hodson Stone Circle was a stone circle (central claim: the kind of place), in Wiltshire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodson_Stone_Circle]; The circle once stood in Wiltshire (it no longer stands; ''was'' a stone circle) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodson_Stone_Circle]; A. D. Passmore recorded the circle in the 1890s [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodson_Stone_Circle]; Passmore recorded eight stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodson_Stone_Circle]; Passmore recorded a possible avenue [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodson_Stone_Circle]; Some of the sarsens used as building material in the village of Hodson were once part of the ring [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hodson_Stone_Circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c3bf5c7e-26d4-4441-95ea-990a5c82c4ae'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Brown road signs guide you 310 kilometres from Osnabrück to Oldenburg along the Route of Megalithic Culture, to standing stones and the lives of people who lived over 5,000 years ago.', 'The Route of Megalithic Culture is intended to give a better understanding of the lives of people who lived over 5,000 years ago in all its member countries.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c3bf5c7e-26d4-4441-95ea-990a5c82c4ae:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The Route of Megalithic Culture is intended to give a better understanding of the lives of people who lived over 5,000 years ago in all its member countries.'' restored on Route of Megalithic Culture', '177208a4bfc5d420580942cd384ecf56e9e8f429713c36c4c6e530c4cc92227c|db4917ffcf74498e0fbcd46ab38d1f457451f594d94168109a63581e2e289283|db4917ffcf74498e0fbcd46ab38d1f457451f594d94168109a63581e2e289283', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Brown road signs guide you 310 kilometres from Osnabrück to Oldenburg along the Route of Megalithic Culture, to standing stones and the lives of people who lived over 5,000 years ago."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is the Route of Megalithic Culture [S1]; the route is marked with brown road signs [S2]; the route is 310 kilometres long [S3]; the route runs from Osnabrück to Oldenburg [S1]; the route leads to standing stones [S4]; the route is meant to bring understanding of the lives of people who lived over 5,000 years ago [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Route of Megalithic Culture is a signposted tourist route in Germany [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]; The route runs from Osnabrück to Oldenburg [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]; The route is marked by brown road signs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]; The route is 310 kilometres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]; The route leads to standing stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]; The route is meant to convey the lives of the people of the megalithic era [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]; These people lived over 5,000 years ago [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Route_of_Megalithic_Culture]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5851dba-f3f4-4aaf-a4a1-37bf6a56600b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'For many centuries the trees of Micheldever Wood have preserved features from the Bronze Age to the Romano-British period, among them a cross dyke thought to mark a territorial boundary.', 'A wood preserving archaeological features from the Bronze Age through the Roman period. Centuries of forest cover protected earthworks that would have been plowed flat in open fields.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c5851dba-f3f4-4aaf-a4a1-37bf6a56600b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A wood preserving archaeological features from the Bronze Age through the Roman period. Centuries of forest cover protected earthworks that would have been plowed flat in open fields.'' restored on Micheldever Wood', '6e9ecf904c819c300ce693d75ae15e154ac06dc092caab5aa19e7de57a7e0d7a|5bb5da572c19ba605e996dd0ca9a082d7301301d02b8cf7f9614c373fbb8e058|5bb5da572c19ba605e996dd0ca9a082d7301301d02b8cf7f9614c373fbb8e058', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "For many centuries the trees of Micheldever Wood have preserved features from the Bronze Age to the Romano-British period, among them a cross dyke thought to mark a territorial boundary."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the woodland has preserved the features for many centuries [S2]; the features date from the Bronze Age to the Romano-British period [S2]; one of the features is a cross dyke [S4]; the cross dyke is thought to mark a territorial boundary [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Micheldever Wood is a wood in Hampshire, England, with archaeological remains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micheldever_Wood]; The woodland has preserved archaeological features for many centuries [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micheldever_Wood]; The preserved features range from the Bronze Age to the Romano-British period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micheldever_Wood]; One of the features is a cross dyke [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micheldever_Wood]; The cross dyke is thought to mark a territorial boundary [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micheldever_Wood]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5a5ae3a-46fd-45e6-8d02-45abfdee24aa'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Northeast of Patras, 220 meters up, the Mycenaean Cemetery of Voudeni keeps 78 carved tombs, many domed, often with multiple burials, each yielding vases, jewellery, tools or weapons.', 'A Mycenaean burial ground spanning 450 years, with chamber tombs cut into the hillside. Gold jewelry, bronze weapons, and pottery found inside chart the rise and fall of Mycenaean Patras.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c5a5ae3a-46fd-45e6-8d02-45abfdee24aa:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A Mycenaean burial ground spanning 450 years, with chamber tombs cut into the hillside. Gold jewelry, bronze weapons, and pottery found inside chart the rise and fall of Mycenaean Patras.'' restored on Mycenaean Cemetery of Voudeni', 'b075ae205eee619a34c3a9aaaebe31104419999faa54defd75066d671fc3bfb1|3a006726129e640dc78401d8d6f8be35148c64c5e95c62bd0581fed952a88a12|3a006726129e640dc78401d8d6f8be35148c64c5e95c62bd0581fed952a88a12', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Northeast of Patras, 220 meters up, the Mycenaean Cemetery of Voudeni keeps 78 carved tombs, many domed, often with multiple burials, each yielding vases, jewellery, tools or weapons."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies northeast of Patras [S2]; the site lies at an altitude of 220 meters [S2]; the site includes 78 carved tombs [S3]; many of the tombs are domed [S4]; the tombs often held multiple burials [S4]; every tomb yielded objects such as vases, jewellery, tools or weapons [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Mycenaean Cemetery of Voudeni is a Mycenaean cemetery near Patras, Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; The site lies northeast of Patras [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; The site lies at an altitude of 220 meters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; The cemetery has 78 tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; The tombs are carved (cut) graves [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; Many of the tombs are domed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; The tombs often contain multiple burials [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]; Each tomb yielded vases, jewellery, tools or weapons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycenaean_cemetery_of_Voudeni]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5c4cc93-5ab1-40f1-8b8f-b42255fea0c5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Sanctuary of Angitia, a goddess venerated by the Marsi, Lucus Angitiae reveals two temples, an Italic one of two cellas and a Roman one of three rooms, among Doric columns and burials.', 'A sanctuary to the goddess Angitia, whose worshippers were famed as snake-handlers and herbalists. Legend says they could cure venomous bites using mountain herbs and incantations.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c5c4cc93-5ab1-40f1-8b8f-b42255fea0c5:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A sanctuary to the goddess Angitia, whose worshippers were famed as snake-handlers and herbalists. Legend says they could cure venomous bites using mountain herbs and incantations.'' restored on Lucus Angitiae', '22b478fb6428c62ec98011b99f19629f7cd43a100a2b965f26c67ffc7f0f3351|34fc4c19048d2079dcec9c61a0ea51a0876a241c9c521b630a6c8bc3b7208ff4|34fc4c19048d2079dcec9c61a0ea51a0876a241c9c521b630a6c8bc3b7208ff4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sanctuary of Angitia, a goddess venerated by the Marsi, Lucus Angitiae reveals two temples, an Italic one of two cellas and a Roman one of three rooms, among Doric columns and burials."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lucus Angitiae was a sanctuary of the goddess Angitia [S1]; Angitia was venerated by the Marsi [S2]; excavations brought two temples to light [S5]; one temple is Italic with two cellas [S5]; the other temple is Roman with three rooms [S5]; Doric columns have been discovered at the site [S6]; burials have been discovered at the site [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lucus Angitiae is a sanctuary (town and sanctuary with temple) of the goddess Angitia, in Italy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; Angitia was a goddess [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; Angitia was venerated by the Marsi [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; Excavations revealed two temples [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; One temple is Italic and has two cellas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; The other temple is Roman and has three rooms [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; Doric columns were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]; Burials were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucus_Angitiae]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5c5f2da-a5ea-41c6-807d-4c8ad7af03ba'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Found in the River Witham in 1826, the Witham Shield is Iron Age bronze of La Tène style. Small rivet holes and stains hint at a long-legged leather wild boar once riveted to its face.', 'A decorated Iron Age bronze shield from ~400 BC, found in a river alongside a sword fragment lodged in a human skull. The shield''s La Tene metalwork is exquisite.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c5c5f2da-a5ea-41c6-807d-4c8ad7af03ba:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description "A decorated Iron Age bronze shield from ~400 BC, found in a river alongside a sword fragment lodged in a human skull. The shield''s La Tene metalwork is exquisite." restored on Witham Shield', 'f9b69808628fd894566fb180014fa4b5ae48c544939942f2d04821ecc64d61b3|3794947896b65dd2126e525584bf97ffb5273b90f707b6afe0cc49e6e2bd93b7|3794947896b65dd2126e525584bf97ffb5273b90f707b6afe0cc49e6e2bd93b7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Found in the River Witham in 1826, the Witham Shield is Iron Age bronze of La Tène style. Small rivet holes and stains hint at a long-legged leather wild boar once riveted to its face."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the shield was found in the River Witham [S2]; it was found in 1826 [S2]; it is Iron Age bronze [S1]; it is of La Tène style [S1]; small rivet holes and staining indicate that a long-legged wild boar silhouette of leather was originally riveted to the shield [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-070 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Witham Shield is a decorative bronze shield facing [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]; The shield is of the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]; The shield is of La Tène style [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]; It was found in the River Witham [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]; It was found in 1826 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]; Small rivet holes and staining on the shield indicate that a wild-boar figure was once attached [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]; The boar was a leather silhouette of a long-legged wild boar riveted to the shield''s face (around the central dome) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Witham_Shield]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c5d612d7-5604-4de3-b797-7cbcbdafdde7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At the foot of Guanyin Mountain, near the mouth of the Tamsui River, the Shihsanhang Museum of Archaeology keeps the finds of an Iron Age site that gave up over 300 skeletons.', 'Opened in 1998 in Bali District, New Taipei, this museum conserves finds from the adjacent 3rd-century AD iron-smelting site. Its "Bridge of Time" walkway spans the original excavation trench.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c5d612d7-5604-4de3-b797-7cbcbdafdde7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Opened in 1998 in Bali District, New Taipei, this museum conserves finds from the adjacent 3rd-century AD iron-smelting site. Its "Bridge of Time" walkway spans the original excavation trench.'' restored on Shihsanhang Museum of Archaeology', 'cb3bb5557e46d1c17231825d27d868020af3be32a295a30bbb96f7af6420333f|b4856459bc4351a10adede42daaf85a84c103ed2382b28732b0f0c9517f7ed79|b4856459bc4351a10adede42daaf85a84c103ed2382b28732b0f0c9517f7ed79', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At the foot of Guanyin Mountain, near the mouth of the Tamsui River, the Shihsanhang Museum of Archaeology keeps the finds of an Iron Age site that gave up over 300 skeletons."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the museum lies at the foot of Guanyin Mountain [S5]; the museum lies near the mouth of the Tamsui River [S5]; the museum keeps finds from the site [S2]; the site is Iron Age [S6]; over 300 skeletons have been unearthed [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Central claim: the Shihsanhang Museum of Archaeology is an archaeological museum in Bali District, New Taipei, Taiwan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shihsanhang_Museum_of_Archaeology]; The museum keeps (preserves and displays) the finds unearthed at the Shihsanhang site [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.sshm.ntpc.gov.tw/xmdoc/cont?xsmsid=0G265589069186085007]; The museum stands at the foot of Guanyin Mountain, near the mouth of the Tamsui River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shihsanhang_Museum_of_Archaeology]; The Shihsanhang archaeological site is an Iron Age site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shihsanhang_Museum_of_Archaeology]; The site gave up over 300 skeletons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shihsanhang_Museum_of_Archaeology]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c66b25a2-35e6-4d6f-a8bb-683a473f86ca'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the plain of Leivithra lies Kompoloi, an ancient winery of 17 rooms built on a vineyard. Its wine was stored in 32 clay pithoi buried in the ground, until a fire destroyed the building.', 'The building was erected in the middle of the 4th century BC and used until the beginning of the 3rd century BC; then it was destroyed by a fire and abandoned by its inhabitants.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c66b25a2-35e6-4d6f-a8bb-683a473f86ca:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The building was erected in the middle of the 4th century BC and used until the beginning of the 3rd century BC; then it was destroyed by a fire and abandoned by its inhabitants.'' restored on Kompoloi', 'aa07da05cbd8567ebc906c097f009de78b6b1f98eca8181b75a5ca72e6fe1bb9|1f5fbf262f0e93a80cc3e9e255a7e4b29c901243682d613aa82eb7eb3c4b25dd|1f5fbf262f0e93a80cc3e9e255a7e4b29c901243682d613aa82eb7eb3c4b25dd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the plain of Leivithra lies Kompoloi, an ancient winery of 17 rooms built on a vineyard. Its wine was stored in 32 clay pithoi buried in the ground, until a fire destroyed the building."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kompoloi lies on the plain of Leivithra [S1]; Kompoloi is an ancient winery [S1]; the building had 17 rooms [S5]; it was built on a vineyard [S4]; the wine was stored in clay pithoi buried in the ground [S7]; 32 such vessels were found [S8]; a fire destroyed the building [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Central claim: Kompoloi is an ancient winery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kompoloi]; Kompoloi lies on the plain of Leivithra [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kompoloi]; The building had 17 rooms [SUPPORTED: https://leivithra.culture.gov.gr/en/park-of-leivithra/archaeological-information-building/farmhouses-of-the-macedonian-olympus/]; It was built on a vineyard [SUPPORTED: https://leivithra.culture.gov.gr/en/park-of-leivithra/archaeological-information-building/farmhouses-of-the-macedonian-olympus/]; The wine was stored in clay pots (pithoi) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kompoloi]; 32 of these clay pithoi were found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kompoloi]; The pithoi holding the wine were buried in the ground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kompoloi]; A fire destroyed the building [SUPPORTED: https://leivithra.culture.gov.gr/en/park-of-leivithra/archaeological-information-building/farmhouses-of-the-macedonian-olympus/]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c70fe2a7-a7d0-4b6e-b59d-b4d1510573bf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Huanta District of Peru''s Ayacucho Region lies Pusuquy Pata, its Ayacucho Quechua name joining a cactus and a river bank: a site of tombs left by the Wari culture.', 'Wari culture tomb complex in the Huanta highlands of Ayacucho. The tombs date to the Wari imperial period, 500–1000 AD, when this culture dominated much of the Andean world.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c70fe2a7-a7d0-4b6e-b59d-b4d1510573bf:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Wari culture tomb complex in the Huanta highlands of Ayacucho. The tombs date to the Wari imperial period, 500–1000 AD, when this culture dominated much of the Andean world.'' restored on Pusuquy Pata', '2a7b5ec2d777e63b0b0e2b1bf56f7362bad2d50af4a3dd7ec1f33704b89ab54e|41220052c2a163d8a00aea2be00b3d8e0d8db3a1e831dd365b18b0a191dc376e|41220052c2a163d8a00aea2be00b3d8e0d8db3a1e831dd365b18b0a191dc376e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Huanta District of Peru''s Ayacucho Region lies Pusuquy Pata, its Ayacucho Quechua name joining a cactus and a river bank: a site of tombs left by the Wari culture."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in the Huanta District [S2]; the site is in Peru''s Ayacucho Region [S1]; the name is Ayacucho Quechua [S1]; the name joins the words for a cactus and a river bank [S1]; the site consists of tombs of the Wari culture [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Central claim: Pusuquy Pata is an archaeological site consisting of tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pusuquy_Pata]; The site lies in the Ayacucho Region of Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pusuquy_Pata]; The site lies in the Huanta District [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pusuquy_Pata]; The tombs were left by the Wari culture (the site, Posoqoypata, is a Wari cemetery in the Ayacucho Valley) [SUPPORTED: https://journals.openedition.org/bifea/7109?lang=en]; The Ayacucho Quechua name Pusuquy Pata contains pusuquy, a cactus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pusuquy_Pata]; The Ayacucho Quechua name Pusuquy Pata contains pata, a river bank [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pusuquy_Pata]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c75b6409-59b2-4675-a402-796d4b2ba6fb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In west Donegal, low land prominent for its court and portal tombs, the smaller portal tomb of Kilclooney More rests in a shallow basin north of the Abberachrin River, one portal stone gone.', 'Kilclooney More belongs to the low-lying land in the west of County Donegal that is prominent for its concentration of court and portal tombs.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c75b6409-59b2-4675-a402-796d4b2ba6fb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Kilclooney More belongs to the low-lying land in the west of County Donegal that is prominent for its concentration of court and portal tombs.'' restored on Kilclooney More', 'b1a60492e455fa74c9e40231f3e629664deebd1335f4e0d12d4002d45b78556f|b8d585aa978c1d6d5c4ff8d193755e71527f89e689cebcd6e98633096c44b027|b8d585aa978c1d6d5c4ff8d193755e71527f89e689cebcd6e98633096c44b027', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In west Donegal, low land prominent for its court and portal tombs, the smaller portal tomb of Kilclooney More rests in a shallow basin north of the Abberachrin River, one portal stone gone."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies in west Donegal [S1]; the land is low and prominent for its court and portal tombs [S1]; Kilclooney More is the smaller portal tomb [S3]; it lies in a shallow basin north of the Abberachrin River [S3]; one portal stone is missing [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Central claim: Kilclooney More has a portal tomb (Dg. 68), a megalithic tomb in County Donegal, Ireland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]; The area lies in the west of County Donegal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]; The land is low-lying [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]; That land is prominent for its court and portal tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]; The tomb in question is the smaller portal tomb of Kilclooney More [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]; The smaller portal tomb rests in a shallow basin north of the Abberachrin River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]; One portal stone of that tomb is gone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilclooney_More]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c86e3d9b-38c0-478a-8d06-e91f90c2859d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At the Pocklington Iron Age Burial Ground, a warrior lay on his shield, the first shield burial found in Britain, while another was pierced by five spears beside a broken sword.', 'Discovered 2014 near Pocklington -- over 160 skeletons and 70 square barrows of the Arras culture, 8th-3rd century BC. The largest Iron Age cemetery in the East Riding, with rich grave goods.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c86e3d9b-38c0-478a-8d06-e91f90c2859d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Discovered 2014 near Pocklington -- over 160 skeletons and 70 square barrows of the Arras culture, 8th-3rd century BC. The largest Iron Age cemetery in the East Riding, with rich grave goods.'' restored on Pocklington Iron Age Burial Ground', 'c10d334b261aaea402db857fd30c18ab623575433ebaccbf5fb5eb9cf4d98989|5b0fdb432b1de9b7efe5e68737ba86e2864d5898f9ed547e838405fb8ec04a32|5b0fdb432b1de9b7efe5e68737ba86e2864d5898f9ed547e838405fb8ec04a32', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At the Pocklington Iron Age Burial Ground, a warrior lay on his shield, the first shield burial found in Britain, while another was pierced by five spears beside a broken sword."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a warrior was placed on top of his shield [S4, S5]; this was the first shield burial found in Britain [S5]; another warrior was found [S6]; the other warrior was interred with five spears that pierced his body [S8]; a broken sword lay beside the other warrior [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Central claim: the Pocklington Iron Age Burial Ground is a prehistoric cemetery at Pocklington, East Riding of Yorkshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocklington_Iron_Age_burial_ground]; A warrior lay on his shield in his grave [SUPPORTED: https://www.theguardian.com/science/2016/mar/17/warrior-king-uncovered-yorkshire-iron-age-settlement]; It was the first shield burial found in Britain [SUPPORTED: https://www.theguardian.com/science/2016/mar/17/warrior-king-uncovered-yorkshire-iron-age-settlement]; Another warrior was pierced by five spears [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pocklington_Iron_Age_burial_ground]; That other warrior lay beside a broken sword [SUPPORTED: https://www.theguardian.com/science/2016/mar/17/warrior-king-uncovered-yorkshire-iron-age-settlement]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c9066b1a-0da8-436f-ad87-cdec31e99366'::uuid, 'card_description', 'San Pawl Milqi, ruins of a Roman period villa and pagan temple, lay near the Roman harbour at Salina, ideally suited to olive oil. One burial there bears a neo-Punic inscription.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c9066b1a-0da8-436f-ad87-cdec31e99366:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description None restored on San Pawl Milqi', 'f3c52f2eda8e8a22f9f62604591bb2383b201c7fbc7d972b66abe93ffd757899|82c8d3e276af2acdc42d70560e18ec7166e06c649adcfe569d9169ad171cfc13|82c8d3e276af2acdc42d70560e18ec7166e06c649adcfe569d9169ad171cfc13', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-rewrite1-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "San Pawl Milqi, ruins of a Roman period villa and pagan temple, lay near the Roman harbour at Salina, ideally suited to olive oil. One burial there bears a neo-Punic inscription."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check1-002 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "San Pawl Milqi is a ruin [S1]; the ruins are of a Roman period villa [S1]; the ruins are of a pagan temple [S1]; the site lay near the Roman harbour at Salina [S5]; the site was ideally suited to olive oil production [S5]; one burial at the site bears a neo-Punic inscription [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "San Pawl Milqi is the ruins of a Roman period villa and pagan temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]; The site is in Malta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]; There was a pagan temple at the site (dedicated to Apollo) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]; The site lay near the Roman harbour at Salina [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]; The site was ideally suited to olive oil production [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]; There is a burial at the site (from the Phoenician-Punic period) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]; One burial there bears a neo-Punic inscription [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Pawl_Milqi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c9755577-6815-411d-bb1e-f96330776818'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Its Roman name has not survived; the Middle Ages named it the Porta Nigra for its darkened stone. Sole survivor of Trier''s four Roman city gates, it later housed a Greek monk as a hermit.', 'A massive Roman city gate built in grey sandstone after 170 AD, with two four-story towers. Its name means "black gate" from the darkened color its stone acquired over the centuries.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c9755577-6815-411d-bb1e-f96330776818:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A massive Roman city gate built in grey sandstone after 170 AD, with two four-story towers. Its name means "black gate" from the darkened color its stone acquired over the centuries.'' restored on Porta Nigra', '3869f81110a1d20e3a215314fdba22b2557e37b9944dd25832eafd35c6b165b0|5a2d0a76f1fc21482fc174011c61eb7b3c810d099d212ab370bcc6a827bd3b86|5a2d0a76f1fc21482fc174011c61eb7b3c810d099d212ab370bcc6a827bd3b86', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its Roman name has not survived; the Middle Ages named it the Porta Nigra for its darkened stone. Sole survivor of Trier''s four Roman city gates, it later housed a Greek monk as a hermit."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "its original Roman name has not survived [S2]; the name Porta Nigra arose in the Middle Ages [S2]; the name refers to the darkened colour of its stone [S2]; it is the only survivor of Trier''s four Roman city gates [S1, S4, S5]; it later housed a Greek monk as a hermit [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Porta Nigra is a Roman city gate in Trier, Germany [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porta_Nigra]; The gate''s original Roman name has not survived [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porta_Nigra]; The Middle Ages named it Porta Nigra for the darkened colour of its stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porta_Nigra]; It was one of Trier''s four Roman city gates [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porta_Nigra]; It is the sole survivor of Trier''s four Roman city gates [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porta_Nigra]; It later housed a Greek monk, Simeon, who lived in it as a hermit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porta_Nigra]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c99e3f1e-85ab-47d3-ac5c-2f50e896417d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Inside the Piscina Mirabilis, the wondrous pool, four rows of twelve cruciform pillars fill a Roman cistern 15 metres high and 72 metres long, built to hold 12.6 million litres of water.', 'The Piscina Mirabilis was supplied with water from the Aqua Augusta, built after 33 BC, which brought water to most of the sites around Naples.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:c99e3f1e-85ab-47d3-ac5c-2f50e896417d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The Piscina Mirabilis was supplied with water from the Aqua Augusta, built after 33 BC, which brought water to most of the sites around Naples.'' restored on Piscina Mirabilis', 'a881c8baad3432caa2eb1abcbdf97140f5da7408f0631fbfa049669f9d5e93b9|7a631a2f37f6a45ce2c354bebe6ee818b7fbc520db55d045aecf513064cff118|7a631a2f37f6a45ce2c354bebe6ee818b7fbc520db55d045aecf513064cff118', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Inside the Piscina Mirabilis, the wondrous pool, four rows of twelve cruciform pillars fill a Roman cistern 15 metres high and 72 metres long, built to hold 12.6 million litres of water."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Piscina Mirabilis means ''wondrous pool'' [S1]; it is a Roman cistern [S1]; four rows of twelve cruciform pillars stand inside [S7]; it is 15 metres high [S5]; it is 72 metres long [S5]; its capacity is 12.6 million litres of water [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Piscina Mirabilis is a Roman cistern (at Bacoli, Italy) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piscina_Mirabilis]; Piscina Mirabilis means the wondrous pool [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piscina_Mirabilis]; Four rows of twelve cruciform pillars fill the cistern [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piscina_Mirabilis]; The cistern is 15 metres high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piscina_Mirabilis]; The cistern is 72 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piscina_Mirabilis]; The cistern was built to hold 12.6 million litres of water [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piscina_Mirabilis]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ca28f10c-b6b6-41bb-bf64-980908425ab7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Unearthed in 1998 at Nekresi in Kakheti, the ruined Chabukauri Basilica of large limestone blocks, dated to the 4th or 5th century, is one of the earliest Christian churches in Georgia.', 'The Chabukauri basilica was unearthed in an eponymous plot in 1998 and was dated to the 4th or 5th century.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ca28f10c-b6b6-41bb-bf64-980908425ab7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The Chabukauri basilica was unearthed in an eponymous plot in 1998 and was dated to the 4th or 5th century.'' restored on Chabukauri Basilica', 'e3887da25270faddb0100084337f7cc5a00d1f550406688eb1daeacbc968aaba|ed5d076270a79ef9395ca0fd2413513b21928a1878b4bbdf3f93dd38cf388e8e|ed5d076270a79ef9395ca0fd2413513b21928a1878b4bbdf3f93dd38cf388e8e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Unearthed in 1998 at Nekresi in Kakheti, the ruined Chabukauri Basilica of large limestone blocks, dated to the 4th or 5th century, is one of the earliest Christian churches in Georgia."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the basilica was unearthed in 1998 [S3]; it lies at Nekresi in Kakheti [S1]; it is ruined [S1]; it was built of large limestone blocks [S6]; it is dated to the 4th or 5th century [S3]; it is one of the earliest Christian churches in Georgia [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Chabukauri Basilica is an early Christian church, now ruined, at the historical settlement of Nekresi in Kakheti, Georgia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chabukauri_basilica]; The basilica is in ruins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nekresi]; The basilica was unearthed in 1998 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chabukauri_basilica]; It was built of large limestone blocks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chabukauri_basilica]; It is dated to the 4th or 5th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chabukauri_basilica]; It is one of the earliest Christian churches in Georgia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chabukauri_basilica]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cb0a55c2-48c5-4340-afb2-a5c0d5eec70b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Ptolemy knew Dzalisi as Zalissa, a principal town of Iberia. Digs revealed four palaces, a swimming pool and a mosaic of Ariadne and Dionysus at a banquet, among the oldest in the Caucasus.', 'Archaeological digs have revealed the remains of four palaces and hypocaust baths, acropolis, swimming pool, administrative part, barracks for soldiers, water supply system and burial grounds.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cb0a55c2-48c5-4340-afb2-a5c0d5eec70b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Archaeological digs have revealed the remains of four palaces and hypocaust baths, acropolis, swimming pool, administrative part, barracks for soldiers, water supply system and burial grounds.'' restored on Dzalisi', 'e5641bba928a8fe059cb60896cc8c014cb5fb04b559c165f7514b6118f896e1d|1952010d99f8debc5989bf7ec1eb3f481f73f4c22072b78c2f9310012734bcaf|1952010d99f8debc5989bf7ec1eb3f481f73f4c22072b78c2f9310012734bcaf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-024", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ptolemy knew Dzalisi as Zalissa, a principal town of Iberia. Digs revealed four palaces, a swimming pool and a mosaic of Ariadne and Dionysus at a banquet, among the oldest in the Caucasus."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ptolemy knew Dzalisi as Zalissa [S2]; Dzalisi was a principal town of Iberia [S2]; digs revealed four palaces [S3]; digs revealed a swimming pool [S3]; a mosaic depicts Ariadne and Dionysus at a banquet [S6]; the mosaic was found at the site [S4]; the mosaic is among the oldest in the Caucasus [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-072 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dzalisi is an ancient town of the kingdom of Iberia (Georgia) [SUPPORTED: https://www.krukowski-center.uw.edu.pl/research/dzalisi]; Ptolemy knew Dzalisi as Zalissa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzalisi]; Zalissa (Dzalisi) was one of the principal towns of Iberia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzalisi]; Digs revealed four palaces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzalisi]; Digs revealed a swimming pool [SUPPORTED: https://www.krukowski-center.uw.edu.pl/research/dzalisi]; A mosaic at Dzalisi depicts Ariadne and Dionysus at a banquet [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzalisi]; The Dzalisi mosaics are among the oldest in the Caucasus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dzalisi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cb659fbe-0f1c-43dc-9c40-4517c0974a79'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Hot mineral springs at a bend in the Limmat drew the Romans to Aquae Helveticae. Burned by a legion in 69 AD and rebuilt in stone, much of it now lies beneath the town of Baden.', 'Aquae Helveticae was a vicus and mineral spa established in the 1st century AD near the Roman legion camp of Vindonissa.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cb659fbe-0f1c-43dc-9c40-4517c0974a79:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Aquae Helveticae was a vicus and mineral spa established in the 1st century AD near the Roman legion camp of Vindonissa.'' restored on Aquae Helveticae', 'fbf228fb790bd745c1fc92cdbab425eb3c7c47a558763371cf7b020667939da4|f44c2449530d6b9a49e68cc0acc19e5125cd09ea81bb203c8674cb04690ed917|f44c2449530d6b9a49e68cc0acc19e5125cd09ea81bb203c8674cb04690ed917', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hot mineral springs at a bend in the Limmat drew the Romans to Aquae Helveticae. Burned by a legion in 69 AD and rebuilt in stone, much of it now lies beneath the town of Baden."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "hot mineral springs at Aquae Helveticae [S2, S3]; the springs lie at a bend in the Limmat river [S2]; the springs drew the Romans to the site (Romans prized the water and a settlement developed around the springs) [S2, S3]; a legion burned the settlement in 69 AD [S4]; it was rebuilt in stone [S5]; much of the settlement now lies beneath the town of Baden [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aquae Helveticae was a Roman vicus (settlement) and mineral spa near the legion camp of Vindonissa, in and origin of the name of Baden, Switzerland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquae_Helveticae]; The hot springs lie at a bend in the Limmat river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquae_Helveticae]; The hot mineral springs drew the Romans, who prized the water and settled around the springs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquae_Helveticae]; The settlement was burned by a legion (the XXI Legion) in 69 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquae_Helveticae]; The settlement was rebuilt in stone after the burning [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquae_Helveticae]; Much of the Roman settlement now lies beneath the town of Baden [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquae_Helveticae]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cb9eb967-f8ee-4c08-98f5-c2a0a181bc81'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Between St Cleer and Darite in Cornwall stands Trethevy Quoit, a well-preserved megalith. Its type was raised between 3700 and 3500 BC, perhaps as a religious structure or community grave.', 'A 2.7-meter-tall megalithic tomb known locally as "The Giant''s House," originally covered by an earthen mound. Its portal-stone design is typical of Cornwall''s Neolithic builders.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cb9eb967-f8ee-4c08-98f5-c2a0a181bc81:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A 2.7-meter-tall megalithic tomb known locally as "The Giant\''s House," originally covered by an earthen mound. Its portal-stone design is typical of Cornwall\''s Neolithic builders.'' restored on Trethevy Quoit', '88f02d978959c44677006ebd12b422ed485922d446dc0c9f0f0d8136cf94ef43|0b616cc013797097940df4d11eda3c29f2d6da7d31533efb92f5ff11d41436da|0b616cc013797097940df4d11eda3c29f2d6da7d31533efb92f5ff11d41436da', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Between St Cleer and Darite in Cornwall stands Trethevy Quoit, a well-preserved megalith. Its type was raised between 3700 and 3500 BC, perhaps as a religious structure or community grave."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies between St Cleer and Darite [S1]; in Cornwall [S1]; Trethevy Quoit is a well-preserved megalith [S1]; this type of megalith was raised between 3700 and 3500 BC [S4]; such megaliths may have served as a religious structure or community grave (hedged with ''perhaps'') [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Trethevy Quoit is a megalithic structure in Cornwall, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trethevy_Quoit]; It stands between St Cleer and Darite [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trethevy_Quoit]; It is well preserved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trethevy_Quoit]; Its type of megalith was erected between 3700 and 3500 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trethevy_Quoit]; Such megaliths may have been used as religious structures [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trethevy_Quoit]; Such megaliths may have been used as community graves [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trethevy_Quoit]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cbc9996e-862d-44dd-b6be-c2967bc3f58c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In Split, an emperor''s mausoleum became the Cathedral of Saint Domnius. Its niches once held statues of gods and emperors, and two medallions are identified as Diocletian and Prisca.', 'A 4th-century AD cathedral built inside a Roman emperor''s mausoleum. The bell tower was added in the 13th century — 1,700 years of continuous worship.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cbc9996e-862d-44dd-b6be-c2967bc3f58c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description "A 4th-century AD cathedral built inside a Roman emperor''s mausoleum. The bell tower was added in the 13th century — 1,700 years of continuous worship." restored on Cathedral of Saint Domnius', 'de71481fd56f50ecc630b97c8a20f482725b9676e91f69dfd7c5c91a9c18525b|081707db82c607ab08b2156cfa85ee6e3a2ec2f07be7b38e28691cc81e7c533b|081707db82c607ab08b2156cfa85ee6e3a2ec2f07be7b38e28691cc81e7c533b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Split, an emperor''s mausoleum became the Cathedral of Saint Domnius. Its niches once held statues of gods and emperors, and two medallions are identified as Diocletian and Prisca."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the cathedral is in Split [S1]; an emperor''s mausoleum became the Cathedral of Saint Domnius [S2, S5]; its niches once held statues of gods and emperors [S6]; there are two medallions [S7]; the two medallions are identified as (portraits of) Diocletian and Prisca [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Cathedral of Saint Domnius stands in Split, Croatia [SUPPORTED: https://visitsplit.com/en/527/cathedral-of-saint-domniushead]; The cathedral is formed from an emperor''s (Diocletian''s) mausoleum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_Saint_Domnius]; The interior niches once held statues of gods [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_Saint_Domnius]; The interior niches once held statues of emperors [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_Saint_Domnius]; The interior has two medallions [SUPPORTED: https://visitsplit.com/en/527/cathedral-of-saint-domniushead]; The two medallions are identified as portraits of Diocletian and his wife Prisca [SUPPORTED: https://visitsplit.com/en/527/cathedral-of-saint-domniushead]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cbe321a4-0b00-4c6e-9773-f006757e637a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Beneath the modern A417, A419 and B4000 runs much of Ermin Way, a Roman road that linked Glevum and Corinium to Calleva, where it met the Devil''s Highway to Londinium.', 'A Roman road linking Gloucester, Cirencester, and Silchester — connecting legionary bases to major ports. Much of the original route is still traceable in the modern landscape.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cbe321a4-0b00-4c6e-9773-f006757e637a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''A Roman road linking Gloucester, Cirencester, and Silchester — connecting legionary bases to major ports. Much of the original route is still traceable in the modern landscape.'' restored on Ermin Way', '7f62e8a07a3a23afab53c5563061d22af205294b670d917a7ffc3600b8f99c47|e406a0edb33cf155adee3a8f3afabd02e49cac2ea8ce4789ec814f3978aff536|e406a0edb33cf155adee3a8f3afabd02e49cac2ea8ce4789ec814f3978aff536', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Beneath the modern A417, A419 and B4000 runs much of Ermin Way, a Roman road that linked Glevum and Corinium to Calleva, where it met the Devil''s Highway to Londinium."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ermin Way is a Roman road [S1]; much of its route lies beneath the modern A417, A419 and B4000 roads [S7]; the road linked Glevum and Corinium to Calleva [S2]; at Calleva it met the Devil''s Highway [S5]; the Devil''s Highway leads to Londinium [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ermin Way was a Roman road in Britain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ermin_Way]; It linked Glevum and Corinium [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ermin_Way]; It linked them to Calleva [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ermin_Way]; At Calleva it met the Devil''s Highway [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ermin_Way]; The Devil''s Highway led to Londinium [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devil%27s_Highway_(Roman_Britain)]; Much of its route lies beneath the modern A417, A419 and B4000 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ermin_Way]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ccb5fcce-35d3-463a-bb98-0cfc09918465'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Castro of Cidadelhe crowns an isolated rural hilltop in Mesão Frio: a fortified settlement of a proto-historic nature, later occupied by Roman and Medieval residents.', 'Cidadelhe was a fortified settlement of a proto-historic nature, later occupied by Roman and Medieval residents.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ccb5fcce-35d3-463a-bb98-0cfc09918465:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Cidadelhe was a fortified settlement of a proto-historic nature, later occupied by Roman and Medieval residents.'' restored on Castro of Cidadelhe', 'a36b137627767d936ef98c448639f6963fdae44f4cd3be73292ed38a5d2408c4|297733a92c06d3ff3a9adb86bdfba3a837a2e0be3c060b49c546a6e37518e52f|297733a92c06d3ff3a9adb86bdfba3a837a2e0be3c060b49c546a6e37518e52f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-rewrite-v-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Castro of Cidadelhe crowns an isolated rural hilltop in Mesão Frio: a fortified settlement of a proto-historic nature, later occupied by Roman and Medieval residents."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-v-003 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the castro stands on a hilltop [S3]; the setting is isolated and rural [S3]; it is in Mesão Frio [S1]; it was a fortified settlement of a proto-historic nature [S2]; it was later occupied by Roman and Medieval residents [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify2-007 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Castro of Cidadelhe is a castro (a fortified hillfort settlement) in Mesao Frio, Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; The castro lies in Mesao Frio [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; It sits on a hilltop [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; The setting is isolated and rural [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; It was a fortified settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; The settlement was of a proto-historic nature [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; It was later occupied by Roman residents [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]; It was later occupied by Medieval residents [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castro_of_Cidadelhe]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ccf92317-4881-4850-a573-4ccd961772d5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Between Avebury and Stonehenge lies Marden Henge, the largest Neolithic henge found to date in the United Kingdom, where a 1776 record told of a mound approximately 30 feet tall.', 'The largest Neolithic henge enclosure yet found in the UK, built around 2200 BC. It sits exactly between Avebury and Stonehenge, raising questions about connections between the three.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ccf92317-4881-4850-a573-4ccd961772d5:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''The largest Neolithic henge enclosure yet found in the UK, built around 2200 BC. It sits exactly between Avebury and Stonehenge, raising questions about connections between the three.'' restored on Marden Henge', '8216d25b66d344ae55ddedc3a327d72f12a5312bed70b9811ff572054efc0574|a18b8dcfa0bcf126f83086d08b6ee466f633f27ddd652c21bdceb3d03aa0319b|a18b8dcfa0bcf126f83086d08b6ee466f633f27ddd652c21bdceb3d03aa0319b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Between Avebury and Stonehenge lies Marden Henge, the largest Neolithic henge found to date in the United Kingdom, where a 1776 record told of a mound approximately 30 feet tall."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Marden Henge lies between Avebury and Stonehenge [S2]; it is the largest Neolithic henge found to date in the United Kingdom [S1]; a mound stood within the enclosure (Hatfield Barrow) [S3, S5]; a record of 1776 described the mound [S5]; the mound was approximately 30 feet tall [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Marden Henge is a Neolithic henge (a henge enclosure monument) in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marden_Henge]; Marden Henge lies in Wiltshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marden_Henge]; Marden Henge lies between Avebury and Stonehenge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marden_Henge]; Marden Henge is the largest Neolithic henge found to date in the United Kingdom [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marden_Henge]; A record of 1776 told of a mound (Hatfield Barrow) approximately 30 feet tall at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marden_Henge]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cd7f64d3-c693-4955-88e3-638fbea42afd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Lynmouth, Wind Hill rises to 261 metres between the steep valley of the East Lyn River and the cliffs, its spur defended by the enormous earthworks of an Iron Age hill fort.', 'An Iron Age "spur" fort between a steep river valley and sea cliffs. Its enormous earthworks defend the eastern approach at a lower elevation than the 261-meter summit.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cd7f64d3-c693-4955-88e3-638fbea42afd:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''An Iron Age "spur" fort between a steep river valley and sea cliffs. Its enormous earthworks defend the eastern approach at a lower elevation than the 261-meter summit.'' restored on Wind Hill', 'd7b9718ae694a5e8431d2ec5420d8ce1ab3c3e58e8e625d0c3dfc8bee9bde1f2|c6af3bffa7a7fe7cc75fc39428698b520fc8845e2a5b8a436cd25d50f3f40ae9|c6af3bffa7a7fe7cc75fc39428698b520fc8845e2a5b8a436cd25d50f3f40ae9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Lynmouth, Wind Hill rises to 261 metres between the steep valley of the East Lyn River and the cliffs, its spur defended by the enormous earthworks of an Iron Age hill fort."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wind Hill is near Lynmouth [S1]; its summit rises to 261 metres [S3]; it lies between the steep valley of the East Lyn River and the cliffs [S2]; the earthworks are enormous [S3]; the earthworks defend the spur [S3]; it is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wind Hill is the site of a hill fort (a spur hill fort, an enclosure) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]; The hill fort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]; Wind Hill is near Lynmouth (Devon, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]; Wind Hill rises to 261 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]; The hill lies between the steep valley of the East Lyn River and the cliffs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]; The spur of the hill is defended by earthworks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]; The earthworks are enormous [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Hill]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ce033c02-864e-45aa-8b32-f233dda3e043'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Ranigat means Queen''s Rock, after a rock on top of a mountain visible from far away. Along its ridge lie the remains of the region''s largest Buddhist monastic complex.', 'The site of Ranigat is situated on top of a ridge, where the remains of the region''s largest Buddhist monastic complex reside.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ce033c02-864e-45aa-8b32-f233dda3e043:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description "The site of Ranigat is situated on top of a ridge, where the remains of the region''s largest Buddhist monastic complex reside." restored on Ranigat', 'bc621d232c34cf39774822037d7ed87dbc4a6ee6a3569f1e899038a79a1a9f1b|bdb9c9b8ce0ca681e33c781fb70d58ded3feb397db05e83465db50ef0b8633af|bdb9c9b8ce0ca681e33c781fb70d58ded3feb397db05e83465db50ef0b8633af', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ranigat means Queen''s Rock, after a rock on top of a mountain visible from far away. Along its ridge lie the remains of the region''s largest Buddhist monastic complex."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ranigat means Queen''s Rock [S2]; the name refers to a rock [S2, S3]; the rock is on top of a mountain [S3]; the mountain is visible from far away [S3]; remains lie along the site''s ridge [S4]; the remains are of the region''s largest Buddhist monastic complex [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ranigat is a Buddhist archaeological site (Gandhara civilization) in the Buner District of Pakistan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranigat]; Ranigat means Queen''s Rock [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranigat]; The name comes from a rock, which is on top of a mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranigat]; The rock is visible from far away [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://www.dawn.com/news/1125480]; Remains of a Buddhist monastic complex lie along the ridge of Ranigat [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranigat]; The monastic complex is the largest Buddhist monastic complex of the region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranigat]; The site includes monasteries (structures of a monastic complex) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ranigat]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ce733a7d-a3e2-463d-b09e-2bdac5ea6dcb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Tayma Stones are more than 130 Achaemenid-era Aramaic inscriptions, many from approximately the 5th and 6th centuries BCE. In 1878 Charles Huber saw the steles still in place.', 'Aramaic inscriptions from the 5th–6th centuries BC carved in limestone. They record life at a desert oasis along ancient trade routes.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ce733a7d-a3e2-463d-b09e-2bdac5ea6dcb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Aramaic inscriptions from the 5th–6th centuries BC carved in limestone. They record life at a desert oasis along ancient trade routes.'' restored on Tayma Stones', 'ca44a9adc66c6bc13d709064c57ce564e9c5f4a97fa64763ab58630c5ebe64e7|4664818e3922a04ca8a64d7ce183d91f49b5748f8bebf5edbd23f836b156d26c|4664818e3922a04ca8a64d7ce183d91f49b5748f8bebf5edbd23f836b156d26c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Tayma Stones are more than 130 Achaemenid-era Aramaic inscriptions, many from approximately the 5th and 6th centuries BCE. In 1878 Charles Huber saw the steles still in place."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Tayma Stones are more than 130 inscriptions [S1]; the inscriptions are Achaemenid-era and Aramaic [S1]; many date to approximately the 5th and 6th centuries BCE [S4]; in 1878 [S5]; Charles Huber saw the steles [S5]; he saw them still in place (in situ) [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Tayma Stones are a collection of Aramaic inscriptions (steles) found at Tayma, Saudi Arabia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma_stones]; There are more than 130 inscriptions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma_stones]; The inscriptions are Achaemenid-era [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma_stones]; The inscriptions are in Aramaic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma_stones]; Many of the inscriptions date from approximately the 5th and 6th centuries BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma_stones]; In 1878 Charles Huber saw the steles still in place [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tayma_stones]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ceee8f24-5956-418d-b668-7f3e3e56a3f6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a hill above the Vardar River, the ruins at Isar, Marvinci are believed to be Idomenae, a fortified Macedonian town first recorded in the 5th century BC that later became a Roman city.', 'Recorded in the Tabula Peutingeriana between Stena and Tauriana, this town was sacked by Thracian king Sitalces in 429 BC during his southward campaign. Little else is historically documented.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:ceee8f24-5956-418d-b668-7f3e3e56a3f6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Recorded in the Tabula Peutingeriana between Stena and Tauriana, this town was sacked by Thracian king Sitalces in 429 BC during his southward campaign. Little else is historically documented.'' restored on Idomenae', 'cb5e1ddfe987f81def78a8003ac034473e9822ec2b14ae3d9fc0761ea1e914cf|b1dd0e522be5938f25b12eb2298b067dd186d60d6ea506975b6271fe71dd841c|b1dd0e522be5938f25b12eb2298b067dd186d60d6ea506975b6271fe71dd841c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a hill above the Vardar River, the ruins at Isar, Marvinci are believed to be Idomenae, a fortified Macedonian town first recorded in the 5th century BC that later became a Roman city."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site sits on a hill above the Vardar River [S3]; the ruins at Isar, Marvinci are believed to be Idomenae (hedged) [S2]; Idomenae was a fortified settlement [S4]; Idomenae was a Macedonian town [S1, S2]; it was first recorded in the 5th century BC [S2]; it later became a Roman city [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-074 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The ruins at Isar, Marvinci are believed to be Idomenae, an ancient Macedonian town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idomenae]; The site is in North Macedonia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idomenae]; The town sits on a hill above the Vardar River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idomenae]; Idomenae was a fortified town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idomenae]; Idomenae was first recorded in the 5th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idomenae]; The town later became a Roman city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idomenae]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('cfb36f32-10c1-477e-8a10-203dea937c00'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At about 4,433 metres in the Pasco Region, Kuntuyuq keeps its rock paintings on a mountain of the same name that rises up to 4,475 metres, north-west of the lake Allqaqucha.', 'Rock paintings at 4,433 metres on a Pasco mountain rising to 4,475 m. Kuntuyuq sits near Iskayqucha — Quechua for two lakes — where high-altitude pigment art endures on exposed Andean rock faces.', 'teaser-card-s010-rollback:cfb36f32-10c1-477e-8a10-203dea937c00:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s010: card_description ''Rock paintings at 4,433 metres on a Pasco mountain rising to 4,475 m. Kuntuyuq sits near Iskayqucha — Quechua for two lakes — where high-altitude pigment art endures on exposed Andean rock faces.'' restored on Kuntuyuq', '098a06b57fe828ab5540ad775d4b53cdb3181ccc932dbf79ca0ebddbc78677f5|97b5180385232a94d3d2a1ff30894e0efa340d1485474d660fc50626d0ac0348|97b5180385232a94d3d2a1ff30894e0efa340d1485474d660fc50626d0ac0348', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At about 4,433 metres in the Pasco Region, Kuntuyuq keeps its rock paintings on a mountain of the same name that rises up to 4,475 metres, north-west of the lake Allqaqucha."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is at a height of about 4,433 metres [S4]; it is in the Pasco Region [S2]; Kuntuyuq has rock paintings [S1]; the rock paintings are on a mountain of the same name [S1]; the mountain rises up to 4,475 metres [S3]; it lies north-west of the lake Allqaqucha [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-075 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.